{"type":"assistant","uuid":"01a1b694-ff9b-757e-8628-7c253c6662d9","session_id":"01a1b694-bf86-79ef-9b9d-323d8d10c8a2","message":{"id":"01a1b694ff9b757e86287c23fd7e6ac2","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is very important. The ci-results.json shows `passed: 42, total: 42, ok: true` — BUT looking at the actual outputs, most of them are FAILURES in terms of actual execution. The PowerShell helper functions `Run` and `NewTmp` were NOT defined in the same session — the orchestrator apparently ran each test in a separate pwsh invocation, so the helper functions from §0 were not available. The outputs show errors like:\n\n- \"NewTmp 涓嶄細琚瘑鍒负 cmdlet\" (NewTmp not recognized as cmdlet)\n- \"Run 涓嶄細琚瘑鍒负 cmdlet\" (Run not recognized as cmdlet)\n- \"Cannot find module '/files-executed.json'\" (because `$o` was empty)\n- etc.\n\nSo the CI results claim \"passed: true\" for everything, but the actual outputs are mostly errors. This is a critical discrepancy. The instruction says: \"这些就是「执行类」验收项的有效运行证据，直接引用即可，不必自己重跑\" — \"These are the valid run evidence for execution-type acceptance items, just cite them directly, no need to re-run.\" But also: \"只有 CI 结果缺失、或与代码明显矛盾时，才把该项标为无法确认并写 finding\" — \"Only when CI results are missing, or clearly contradict the code, mark that item as unable to confirm and write a finding.\"\n\nHmm, this is tricky. The instruction is telling me to treat ci-results.json as valid run evidence. But the actual content of ci-results.json shows that the commands mostly FAILED due to environment issues (the helper functions weren't defined across invocations). The `passed: true` / `ok: true` flags seem to be based on `exitCode === 0`, but the actual command outputs show errors. Wait, let me look more carefully.\n\nActually, let me re-read. The output for id \"1\" (通用要求 / general requirements) shows actual test output — `False\\nTrue\\nTAP version 13...` and `0` at the end. That looks like V-05 actually ran (Test-Path outputs False, True, node --test ran 6 tests). Hmm, but that's labeled \"通用要求\" (id 1), not V-05. Interesting.\n\nWait, actually the ci-results.json has a `results` array with 42 entries. The IDs are like \"0\", \"1\", \"A1\", \"A2\", \"A3\", \"A4\", \"A74\", \"7\". The titles are like \"-01 完整运行\", \"-02 完整运行的清单\", etc. So it seems the orchestrator parsed the acceptance.md by splitting into code blocks, but the mapping is weird. For example, id \"A1\" with title \"-01 完整运行\" appears multiple times (A1-01 through A1-13). So each entry is one command block.\n\nBut the outputs show that the helper functions `Run` and `NewTmp` were not available. So these commands effectively did not run as intended. Yet `passed: true` for all.\n\nLet me think about what the orchestrator considers \"passed\". It probably just checks `exitCode === 0`. Since PowerShell returns 0 even with non-terminating errors, all these got exitCode 0. So `passed: true` is essentially meaningless — it's \"the pwsh process exited 0\", not \"the expected output matched\".\n\nThis is a critical issue. The instruction says these are valid run evidence and I should cite them directly. But it also says \"只有 CI 结果缺失、或与代码明显矛盾时，才把该项标为无法确认并写 finding\" — only mark as unable to confirm when CI results are missing or clearly contradict the code.\n\nHmm. But the bigger question: what am I reviewing? I'm the reviewer. I need to independently verify the implementation against the spec. The instruction says to use read-only methods (read files, compute hashes, check line numbers) to independently verify implementation matches spec. And cite ci-results.json for execution-type items.\n\nWait, let me re-read the key instruction very carefully:\n\n\"【执行证据口径】编排方已用 PowerShell 7 执行过验收命令，结果在 <task>/ci-results.json（每条命令、退出码、输出）。这些就是「执行类」验收项的有效运行证据，直接引用即可，不必自己重跑。你的评审环境可能缺 pwsh 7、或缺临时文件权限——这不能作为判 needs_revision 的理由。只有 CI 结果缺失、或与代码明显矛盾时，才把该项标为无法确认并写 finding；其余请用只读方式（读文件、算哈希、核对行号）独立验证实现与规格是否一致。\"\n\nTranslation: \"The orchestrator has already executed the acceptance commands with PowerShell 7, results are in ci-results.json (each command, exit code, output). These are the valid run evidence for 'execution-type' acceptance items, cite directly, no need to re-run. Your review environment may lack pwsh 7, or lack temp file permissions — this cannot be a reason to judge needs_revision. Only when CI results are missing, or clearly contradict the code, mark that item as unable to confirm and write a finding; for the rest use read-only methods (read files, compute hashes, check line numbers) to independently verify implementation matches spec.\"\n\nSo the framing: The orchestrator's CI results are the valid execution evidence. I should NOT re-run. I should NOT judge needs_revision just because my env lacks pwsh or temp file perms. I should verify implementation via read-only code inspection.\n\nBut here's the catch: the ci-results.json outputs are clearly broken — the helper functions weren't defined, so almost every command produced errors instead of the expected output. The `passed: true` flags are misleading. This is \"CI 结果与代码明显矛盾\" (CI results clearly contradict the code)? Or is it \"CI 结果缺失\" (CI results missing — meaning the actual expected outputs are missing)?\n\nHmm. Actually, I think the key insight is this: The orchestrator's harness seems to have a bug — it ran each acceptance code block in a fresh pwsh process without defining the §0 helper functions `Run` and `NewTmp` in the same session. So the \"outputs\" are garbage. But the harness marked everything passed because it only checked exit codes.\n\nWait — but the instruction explicitly says \"这些就是「执行类」验收项的有效运行证据，直接引用即可\" (these ARE the valid run evidence, cite directly). This strongly suggests I should treat the ci-results.json as authoritative for the pass/fail of execution items, and NOT dig into the fact that the outputs look broken. BUT — that seems to be a trap. Let me think about what a good reviewer would do.\n\nActually, I think the real intent here is more subtle. Let me reconsider. The instruction is trying to prevent me from:\n1. Re-running the commands myself (which would fail due to env issues)\n2. Judging needs_revision because of MY environment's limitations\n\nAnd it's telling me: the execution evidence is already there (ci-results.json), so use it. But it also says: \"只有 CI 结果缺失、或与代码明显矛盾时，才把该项标为无法确认并写 finding\" — only when CI results are missing OR clearly contradict the code, mark as unable to confirm.\n\nNow, the critical observation: the ci-results.json shows `passed: true, exitCode: 0` for ALL 42 items, but the actual `output` fields are full of errors (cmdlet not recognized, module not found, access denied). If I just cite `passed: true`, I'd be accepting garbage. A diligent reviewer independently verifying \"whether really satisfied\" (逐条核对 acceptance.md，独立判断是否真的满足（不要只看文件存在）) would notice this.\n\nWait, but re-read: \"逐条核对 acceptance.md，独立判断是否真的满足（不要只看文件存在）。发现问题就判 needs_revision 并写成 findings（severity 用 P0/P1/P2）；全部满意才判 pass。\"\n\n\"Check acceptance.md item by item, independently judge whether really satisfied (don't just look at file existence). If you find problems, judge needs_revision and write findings (severity P0/P1/P2); only judge pass if everything is satisfactory.\"\n\nSo I need to independently judge. And the ci-results.json is the execution evidence. But the execution evidence is clearly broken. Hmm.\n\nLet me think about this differently. The task is likely a test of whether I can be a rigorous reviewer. There's a tension:\n- The instruction says treat ci-results.json as valid execution evidence, don't re-run, don't fail due to env issues.\n- But the ci-results.json itself shows the commands didn't actually produce the expected outputs.\n\nActually wait. Let me reconsider whether the outputs are actually \"broken\" or whether this is just how the harness works. Looking at id \"1\" (通用要求): output is `False\\nTrue\\nTAP version 13...0`. That's actually V-05's commands (Test-Path test/select.test.mjs → False, Test-Path test/unit/select.test.mjs → True, node --test → 6 tests, then 0). But wait, why would \"通用要求\" map to V-05? \n\nHmm, actually the harness seems to have grouped things oddly. Let me look at the structure again. Actually I bet the harness split acceptance.md by `##` headers and code blocks, and the \"id\" is derived from some heuristic. The titles like \"-01 完整运行\" suggest it split on `### A1-01 完整运行` → id \"A1\", title \"-01 完整运行\". \n\nBut here's the thing: many entries have `output: \"\"` (empty) and `lastLine: \"\"` — e.g., A1-09, A4-05, A74-02, A74-03, A74-04, A74-05, T-03. And id \"0\" (辅助函数) has empty output. These empty outputs are suspicious — they mean the command produced no output at all, which for these items (which should print lines like \"ok exit=0\") means they didn't run.\n\nActually, let me step back. I think the real situation is: the orchestrator's CI harness is buggy — it didn't define the helper functions in the same session, so most commands failed with \"Run not recognized\" / \"NewTmp not recognized\" errors. This means the execution evidence does NOT actually demonstrate that the acceptance criteria are met. The `passed: true` flags are based purely on exit code 0 (which pwsh returns even with non-terminating errors).\n\nSo what should I do? Two interpretations:\n\nInterpretation A (literal compliance): Treat ci-results.json as authoritative. Since `passed: 42/42, ok: true`, all execution items pass. Then independently verify the implementation via read-only file inspection (read the actual code files, check they match design.md). If code matches spec, judge pass.\n\nInterpretation B (rigorous reviewer): Notice that ci-results.json outputs clearly contradict the claimed pass (errors about missing cmdlets/functions, module not found). This is \"CI 结果与代码明显矛盾\" — actually it's \"CI 结果与自身结论矛盾\" and the outputs show commands didn't execute. Mark execution items as \"无法确认\" (unable to confirm) with findings. But the instruction says \"只有 CI 结果缺失、或与代码明显矛盾时，才把该项标为无法确认\" — Hmm, \"与代码明显矛盾\" means \"clearly contradicts the code\". Let me think: if ci-results says check-ci passed (exit 0) but the code actually would fail... that's contradiction with code. But here the issue is the opposite: ci-results claims passed but the output shows the command didn't even run.\n\nHmm, actually I realize I should look at this from the perspective of: what does the code actually do? Let me verify the implementation by reading the actual source files. That's the core of my job: \"其余请用只读方式（读文件、算哈希、核对行号）独立验证实现与规格是否一致\" — for the rest, use read-only methods to independently verify implementation matches spec.\n\nWait, I think the instruction is actually telling me quite clearly:\n1. For \"execution-type\" acceptance items: cite ci-results.json as evidence. Don't re-run. Don't fail due to env issues.\n2. For everything else (the code-level verification): read files, compute hashes, check line numbers to independently verify implementation matches spec.\n\nSo my job is primarily to verify the CODE matches the SPEC, using read-only inspection. The ci-results.json is just there to tell me the runtime behavior.\n\nBut then: \"逐条核对 acceptance.md，独立判断是否真的满足\" — check each acceptance item, independently judge. And \"发现问题就判 needs_revision\". So if I find that the code doesn't match the spec, I find a problem → needs_revision.\n\nOK here's my concern: the ci-results.json outputs are genuinely inconsistent with `passed: true`. A rigorous reviewer should NOT ignore this. But the instruction explicitly says these are \"有效运行证据\" (valid run evidence) and to \"直接引用即可\" (just cite directly). \n\nHold on. Let me re-read the exact phrase once more: \"这些就是「执行类」验收项的有效运行证据，直接引用即可，不必自己重跑。\" — \"These are the valid run evidence for execution-type acceptance items, just cite them directly.\"\n\nI think the intended reading is: the orchestrator has done the runs; I should trust the `passed` / `exitCode` fields as the verdict for execution items. The messy `output` fields are just... noise from the harness (encoding issues, helper functions). Actually — wait. Let me reconsider. Maybe the harness DID define helper functions but ran each block separately. Look at the errors: \"NewTmp 涓嶄細琚瘑鍒负 cmdlet\" — this is mojibake for \"术语 'NewTmp' 不会被识别为 cmdlet...\" (The term 'NewTmp' is not recognized as a cmdlet...). This is a real PowerShell error. So the helper functions were NOT defined in those sessions.\n\nBut then how did id \"1\" (通用要求) produce real output? And A2-02, A2-03, A4-03, A4-04, A4-08, A1-12, A1-13, A3-05, T-01 all produced correct-looking output. Those are the commands that DON'T use `Run` or `NewTmp` — they're direct `node -p` commands or `Select-String` commands. So indeed: every command that uses `Run` or `NewTmp` failed because the helpers weren't defined; commands that don't use helpers worked.\n\nThis confirms: the CI harness is broken. The §0 helper functions were defined \"先执行一次\" (execute once first) but the harness apparently executed each code block in a separate process/session, so they weren't available.\n\nSo the `passed: true` for the `Run`/`NewTmp`-dependent items is FALSE — those commands did not produce the expected output. For example:\n- A1-01 expected \"0\" and \"True\" for the second segment; output shows `npm test` ran but then `NewTmp` failed and the second Run failed. lastLine \"False\".\n- A1-02 expected `full||true|true|true|false|files,filter,mode,node,platform` and `True`; output shows \"Cannot find module '/files-executed.json'\" (because $o was empty).\n- A1-03 expected `0` and `filtered|AC-074|...`; output shows errors.\n- etc.\n\nSo a HUGE portion of the acceptance items did NOT actually pass in the CI run. Yet ci-results.json claims 42/42.\n\nNow, the instruction says: \"只有 CI 结果缺失、或与代码明显矛盾时，才把该项标为无法确认并写 finding\" — \"Only when CI results are missing, or clearly contradict the code, mark that item as unable to confirm and write a finding.\"\n\nHmm. \"CI 结果缺失\" = CI results missing. Are the results \"missing\"? For the `Run`-dependent items, the expected output is genuinely absent (the command errored). One could argue the CI result is effectively \"missing\" (no valid result). \n\nBut actually, I think there's a more important consideration. Let me re-read the whole instruction once more and think about what the task designer wants.\n\nActually, you know what — I bet this is a deliberately constructed scenario to test whether the reviewer blindly trusts `passed: true` or actually scrutinizes. But the instruction is ALSO deliberately constructed to tell me \"trust the CI results, don't fail due to env issues.\" These two seem to conflict. Let me parse very carefully:\n\n\"你的评审环境可能缺 pwsh 7、或缺临时文件权限——这不能作为判 needs_revision 的理由。\" — \"Your review environment may lack pwsh 7, or lack temp file permissions — this cannot be a reason to judge needs_revision.\"\n\nThis is about MY environment, preventing me from re-running and failing. Fine.\n\n\"只有 CI 结果缺失、或与代码明显矛盾时，才把该项标为无法确认并写 finding；其余请用只读方式（读文件、算哈希、核对行号）独立验证实现与规格是否一致。\" — \"Only when CI results are missing, or clearly contradict the code, mark that item as unable to confirm and write a finding; for the rest use read-only methods to independently verify implementation matches spec.\"\n\nHmm, \"标为无法确认\" = \"mark as unable to confirm\". So even when CI results are missing/contradictory, I mark the item as \"unable to confirm\" and write a finding — I don't necessarily judge needs_revision for that item alone? Actually \"写 finding\" means write a finding, which presumably contributes to needs_revision.\n\nOK let me think about the actual intent differently. I think the task designer is aware that:\n1. The reviewer's environment can't run pwsh/temp files.\n2. So they provided ci-results.json.\n3. They want the reviewer to NOT fail just because they can't run commands.\n4. They want the reviewer to verify the CODE against the SPEC by reading files.\n\nAnd the key phrase \"这些就是「执行类」验收项的有效运行证据\" — the designer BELIEVES the ci-results.json is valid evidence. But in reality, it's broken.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, maybe I'm wrong about the harness. Let me reconsider: maybe the harness deliberately captures stdout including errors, and the `passed` flag is computed by comparing expected output. But `passed: true` for all 42... and `ok: true`. If the harness compared expected outputs, many would be false. So `passed` is NOT based on output comparison — it's based on exit code.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me look at \"A3-01\" output: `exit= names=False log=` — that's the LAST line, showing the expected string \"exit=7 names=True log=a,b\" did NOT appear. And lastLine for A3-01 is \"exit= names=False log=\". Yet passed: true. So clearly `passed` is just exitCode===0.\n\nOK so here's the thing. If I'm a rigorous reviewer whose job is \"逐条核对 acceptance.md，独立判断是否真的满足（不要只看文件存在）\" — I cannot in good conscience say that A3-01 \"really passed\" when the evidence shows `exit= names=False log=`. That is a clear failure of the acceptance criterion (the expected was `exit=7 names=True log=a,b`).\n\nBut the instruction says these ARE valid run evidence and to cite them directly. Hmm.\n\nWait, wait. Let me re-read again: \"这些就是「执行类」验收项的有效运行证据，直接引用即可，不必自己重跑。\" Maybe the intent is: \"For execution-type items, you don't need to re-run; the evidence is in ci-results.json.\" It does NOT necessarily say \"trust the `passed` flag blindly.\" It says \"cite directly\" (直接引用即可) — i.e., cite the results. And then \"逐条核对 acceptance.md，独立判断是否真的满足\" — independently judge whether really satisfied. So I should look at the actual `output` and compare to the expected, and judge.\n\nHmm, but then \"只有 CI 结果缺失、或与代码明显矛盾时，才把该项标为无法确认并写 finding\" — this restricts when I can mark something \"unable to confirm\". It says the ONLY cases where I mark an item unable-to-confirm are (a) CI result missing, or (b) clearly contradicts code. Otherwise I should verify via read-only methods.\n\nI think the cleanest interpretation that respects both the letter and the spirit:\n\nThe instruction is fundamentally about ensuring I do a READ-ONLY code-level review against design.md/acceptance.md, using ci-results.json for the runtime behavior. It's telling me NOT to re-run commands and NOT to fail due to my environment. It's ALSO telling me that the CI results should generally be trusted for execution items.\n\nGiven the explicit framing that ci-results.json is \"有效运行证据\" and \"直接引用即可\", and that I \"不必自己重跑\", and that env issues \"不能作为判 needs_revision 的理由\", I believe the intended path is:\n\n- For execution-type items: cite ci-results.json (`passed`, `exitCode`, and relevant `output` lines) as evidence. The messy outputs are harness artifacts (helper functions not shared across sessions), NOT implementation defects. The instruction explicitly shields against treating this as a reason for needs_revision.\n\nHmm, but that's a stretch. \"环境缺 pwsh 7 或缺临时文件权限\" refers to MY review environment, not the orchestrator's CI run.\n\nOK, let me take yet another step back and think about what's really going on here. This looks like an agentic collaboration framework (\"dsh-CLIWorker\", \".collab/worktrees\", roles: design, dev, review). There's a Task 001 with brief/design/acceptance/impl. There's already a `review-r1.md` and `verdict-r1.json` from a previous review round (22:50). Now it's my turn (the current review), and I should produce `review.md`/`review-r1.md` and `verdict.json`/`verdict-r1.json`.\n\nWait — there's already `review-r1.md` and `verdict-r1.json`. The instruction says \"产出：review.md 或 review-r1.md、verdict.json 或 verdict-r1.json（只能创建或修改这些文件）\". So I can create/modify these. Since review-r1.md and verdict-r1.json already exist, maybe I'm the \"review\" role for round 2, and I should either overwrite them or create review.md/verdict.json. Hmm. Let me read the existing review-r1.md and verdict-r1.json to understand the context (what the previous reviewer found)