b1aec via reading .git files. But I'm told NOT to execute git commands. I can read .git files directly (read-only). Let me check state.json etc. — they may contain the sha.\n\nLet me also reconsider: \"verdict 必须写明 so 验证的 git sha\" — this might be \"写明所验证的 git sha\" (write the verified git sha). Or possibly \"so\" refers to a specific file/tool. Hmm, in the previous verdict, the field is \"sha\". Let me check state.json for a \"sha\" field.\n\nActually, I wonder if \"so\" is a typo for \"本轮\" or something. Or it could be a label like \"so\" = an agent name (e.g., a role \"so\" = \"solution operator\"?). Hmm. Actually maybe \"so\" refers to \"编排方\" (orchestrator) — no. \n\nWait — maybe it's a literal requirement: \"写明 so 验证的 git sha\" = \"write down the git sha verified by `so`\". Perhaps there's a tool/role called \"so\" that verifies the sha. Hmm. Or maybe it means the sha should be the one \"so\" (thus) verified — i.e., the sha that I verified through read-only means. I think \"所验证的\" (the verified) is the most plausible reading — a pinyin typo: \"所\" (suo) typed as \"so\". I'll go with: record the verified git sha.\n\nLet me read state.json, run.json, notice.json, live.json, and the handoff directory.","signature":""}],"model":"hy4","role":"assistant","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":null,"cache_read_input_tokens":null,"cache_creation":null,"server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":null}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"_requestId":"01a1b694c37777a89e7b949da7c8c488","_messageId":"01a1b69609a578c282e8583c1d4f7aed","_meta":{"codebuddy.ai/conversationRequestId":"01a1b694c37777a89e7b949da7c8c488","codebuddy.ai/modelRequestId":"01a1b69609a578c282e8583c1d4f7aed","codebuddy.ai/llmMessageId":"01a1b69609a578c282e8583c1d4f7aed","codebuddy.ai/messageId":"01a1b696-09a5-78c2-82e8-583ead333856","traceparent":"00-f2afc752a8a37e0cd10f85162fee58ac-07ea269c5c5feea8-01","baggage":"codebuddy.ses