{"type":"assistant","uuid":"01a1b69a-a997-704d-9a51-7f0b64021d93","session_id":"01a1b694-bf86-79ef-9b9d-323d8d10c8a2","message":{"id":"01a1b69aa997704d9a517f09751cb786","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The 12 \"differences\" are purely CRLF in the worktree vs LF in the HEAD tree — an original checkout artifact, not modifications by dev. All equal after normalization. So the write-scope check is effectively clean: no file outside the allowed list has real content changes, no untracked files outside exempt directories, no repo artifacts (test-results/, files-executed.json, skips.jsonl).\n\nNote: design.md §1 says `.gitignore` isn't in write_scope and can't be changed. It has CRLF in the worktree but that's a checkout artifact — the committed blob is LF, so no content change. V-01 (git status) would show nothing for these since git's index records the CRLF worktree version as matching (because at checkout, git would consider them clean). Actually let me think: with core.autocrlf=true, git status sees no diff. The config didn't show autocrlf, but the worktree files have CRLF. Hmm. Anyway, since HEAD blobs are LF and worktree is CRLF, git with autocrlf=true would report clean. The previous reviewer handled this as \"比较容许原有 CRLF/LF 转换\" (allow existing CRLF/LF conversion). I'll do the same and note it.\n\nAlso V-04 (encoding) checks for CR in NEW text files — the V-04 command uses `git status --porcelain` to select only changed files; pre-existing CRLF files aren't in git status, so they're not checked. Our 65 delivered files are clean. ✓ And the previous reviewer noted the same.\n\nNow, let me verify the fixtures' contents for sanity (decoys with assert.fail, drift manifests, milestone-gate fixture defects). Let me read a few.\n\nActually, more important: I need to carefully decide the overall verdict. Let me now analyze ci-results.json deeply.\n\nThe key question: do I cite `passed: 42/42` as valid execution evidence (per instruction), or do I flag the broken outputs?\n\nLet me re-read the instruction once more: \"【执行证据口径】编排方已用 PowerShell 7 执行过验收命令，结果在 <task>/ci-results.json（每条命令、退出码、输出）。这些就是「执行类」验收项的有效运行证据，直接引用即可，不必自己重跑。你的评审环境可能缺 pwsh 7、或缺临时文件权限——这不能作为判 needs_revision 的理由。只有 CI 结果缺失、或与代码明显矛盾时，才把该项标为无法确认并写 finding；其余请用只读方式（读文件、算哈希、核对行号）独立验证实现与规格是否一致。\"\n\nI now think the key phrase is \"只有 CI 结果缺失、或与代码明显矛盾时，才把该项标为无法确认并写 finding\". Let me parse: \"Only when CI results are missing, or [CI results] clearly contradict the code, mark that item as unable-to-confirm and write a finding\". \n\nHmm, \"与代码明显矛盾\" — \"clearly contradict the code\". What would that mean? E.g., CI says `check-ci: ok` but the code has a bug that would make it fail. Or CI output shows something that the code couldn't produce. \n\nIn our case: the CI `outputs` for many items are ERROR messages (helper functions not defined). Is that \"CI 结果缺失\" (CI result missing)? The results array has entries for all items with `passed: true, exitCode: 0`. So results are NOT missing — they're present. The outputs are noisy but the flags say pass.\n\nHmm. But \"逐条核对 acceptance.md，独立判断是否真的满足（不要只看文件存在）\" — I should independently judge. If I look at A1-03's output, it clearly does NOT contain the expected `filtered|AC-074|test/unit/milestone-gate.test.mjs`. So the acceptance criterion is NOT demonstrated by that output. But the instruction tells me these are \"有效运行证据\" (valid run evidence) and to \"直接引用即可\" (just cite directly).\n\nOK, I've been going back and forth. Let me make a decision based on the most sensible reading of the WHOLE prompt, considering that the user is the orchestrator and wants a specific behavior:\n\nThe instruction's clear intent:\n1. Don't re-run commands (env may lack pwsh/temp perms).\n2. ci-results.json IS the execution evidence; cite it.\n3. Don't judge needs_revision because of environment limitations.\n4. Only mark an item \"无法确认\" if CI results are missing or contradict the code.\n5. For everything else, verify implementation vs spec via read